{"causes": ["The ongoing Achaemenid Persian threat to Greek independence necessitated strong Spartan military leadership.", "The deposition of the previous Eurypontid king, Demaratus, created a vacancy for Leotychidas to ascend to the Spartan throne in 491 BCE.", "Sparta's dual monarchy system, requiring two kings from the Agiad and Eurypontid houses, ensured Leotychidas's position as co-ruler."], "effects": ["The decisive Greek victory at the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE shattered Persian naval supremacy in the Aegean Sea.", "The liberation of Ionian Greek cities from Persian rule fostered an environment conducive to the rise of Athenian hegemony and the Delian League.", "His exile to Tegea in 476 BCE due to bribery charges contributed to a period of internal political instability within Sparta."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "demaratus", "sourceName": "Demaratus", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "leotychidas-ii", "targetName": "Leotychidas II", "context": "Leotychidas II succeeded Demaratus as Eurypontid king of Sparta in 491 BCE after Demaratus's deposition."}, {"sourceSlug": "leotychidas-ii", "sourceName": "Leotychidas II", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-mycale", "targetName": "Battle of Mycale", "context": "Leotychidas II commanded the combined Greek fleet and land forces at the decisive Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "leotychidas-ii", "sourceName": "Leotychidas II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "ionia", "targetName": "Ionia", "context": "Leotychidas II campaigned in Ionia to liberate Greek cities from Persian rule following the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "persian-wars", "sourceName": "Persian Wars", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "leotychidas-ii", "targetName": "Leotychidas II", "context": "Leotychidas II was a key Spartan commander during the latter stages of the Persian Wars, particularly from 490–478 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "delian-league", "sourceName": "Delian League", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "leotychidas-ii", "targetName": "Leotychidas II", "context": "The victory at Mycale, largely due to Leotychidas II, directly paved the way for the formation of the Athenian-led Delian League in 478 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Sparta, Greece", "role": "Seat of Kingship"}, {"name": "Mycale, Ionia", "role": "Major Battle Site"}, {"name": "Tegea, Greece", "role": "Place of Exile"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Leotychidas II", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "leotychidas-ii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q360151", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leotychidas_II"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Leotychidas II's command at the Battle of Mycale in 479 BCE was a pivotal moment, irrevocably ending Persian naval dominance in the Aegean and directly enabling the rise of Athenian hegemony through the Delian League, thus reshaping the geopolitical landscape of Classical Greece.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-04T17:41:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30934558671", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Leotychidas II was king of Sparta from 491–476 BC, alongside Cleomenes I and later Leonidas I and Pleistarchus. He led Spartan forces during the Persian Wars from 490–478 BC.", "newValue": "Leotychidas II, a king from the Eurypontid house of Sparta, reigned from 491 to 468 BCE, serving alongside Agiad kings Cleomenes I, Leonidas I, and Pleistarchus. His tenure was defined by the pivotal latter stages of the Greco-Persian Wars, where he emerged as a crucial military commander whose actions fundamentally shifted the balance of power in the Aegean Sea. His leadership was instrumental in securing Greek independence against the vast Achaemenid Empire, particularly through decisive naval and land engagements that marked the end of major Persian incursions into the Greek world.

His most celebrated achievement occurred in 479 BCE when, commanding the united Greek fleet and land forces, he orchestrated the crushing victory at the Battle of Mycale, fought simultaneously with the Battle of Plataea. This engagement, near the Ionian coast, saw the destruction of the Persian fleet and a significant portion of their land army, effectively ending Persian naval power in the Aegean. Following this triumph, Leotychidas led expeditions to liberate Greek cities in Ionia and the Hellespont and later campaigned in Thessaly against the pro-Persian Aleuadae. However, his career ended ignominiously; in 476 BCE, he was accused of accepting a bribe from the Aleuadae during his Thessalian campaign and fled into exile in Tegea, avoiding a Spartan trial.

